  "worked_solution": "Let's denote:\n\n- $P(n):$ ${n}^{2}$ is odd\n\n- $Q(n):$ $n$ is odd\n\nThen, the given sentence has the following logical structure:\n\n$$\nP(n)\u21d2Q(n)\n$$\n\nThis, in turn, is equivalent to \n\n$$\n\u2200\\,n\\in U,\\,P(n)\u21d2Q(n)\n$$\n\nwhere the universal set $U$ is usually apparent from the context. In this case, $n\\in N.$\n\nTherefore, using formal language, our statement reads \n\nFor all natural number $n,$ if ${n}^{2}$ is odd, then $n$ is odd.\n\nOr, more formally:\n\n$\u2200\\,n\\in N,\\,$ (${n}^{2}$ is odd) $\u21d2$ ($n$ is odd)",
